      <h1 class="aip-title">The Golden Age</h1>
      <p class="aip-subtitle">The Promise, the Crusader, and the Plan</p>
      <div class="aip-rule"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
    </header>

    <div class="aip-body">
      <p>America’s buildings, statues, and money carry signs from old Rome. Those signs are not only in stone. Today they show up in speeches, on the bodies of leaders, and in the plans that run the government. This article looks at three of them: a promise, a set of tattoos, and a plan. All three lead back to Rome.</p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">The Promise</h2>
      <p>On January 20, 2025, President Trump stood at his second swearing-in and said, "The golden age of America begins right now." He has said "golden age" many times since. Most people heard it as good news. But the phrase is very old, and it did not start in America.</p>
      <p>The ancient Greeks taught that history moves through ages named after metals: gold, then silver, then bronze, then iron. The golden age was the best time, when the gods were pleased with men. Rome took that idea and used it to sell its emperors. A Roman poet named Virgil wrote that a new golden age was being born. Caesar Augustus then threw a huge public festival in 17 BC to announce that the new age had begun under him. After that, almost every Roman emperor claimed that his rule was the golden age.</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>When a ruler announces a golden age, he is saying that history now turns around him.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">It Is Already on Our Money</h2>
      <p>Turn over a <a class="aip-inline" href="/american-idolatry/dollar-bill">one-dollar bill</a>. Under the pyramid are the Latin words <em>Novus Ordo Seclorum</em>. They mean "a new order of the ages." That line was taken from the very same poem by Virgil that promised Rome its golden age.</p>
      <p>Rome also put this idea on its coins. Roman coins showed the phoenix, a bird said to burn up and rise again from its own ashes. The message was that Rome would renew itself forever. When a President today promises a golden age, he is saying out loud what has been printed on the dollar since the beginning.</p>
      <p>Scripture already speaks to golden ages. Daniel told King Nebuchadnezzar, "Thou art this head of gold" (Daniel 2:38). In the very next chapter that king built a golden statue ninety feet tall and ordered everyone to bow to it (Daniel 3:1-6). Tell a ruler he is the golden age, and he soon wants to be worshipped for it.</p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">The Tattoos</h2>
      <p>Pete Hegseth runs the nation’s military. Since 2025 his title has been Secretary of War. His body is covered in symbols from the Crusades, the holy wars the Roman church fought in the Middle Ages.</p>
      <ul class="aip-list">
        <li>On his chest is a large Jerusalem cross. It was the flag of the kingdom the Crusaders took by the sword.</li>
        <li>On his arm are the words <em>Deus Vult</em>, Latin for "God wills it." That was the battle cry shouted when the Pope launched the First Crusade in 1095.</li>
        <li>On his shoulder is the Chi-Rho, two Greek letters joined together. The Roman emperor Constantine painted it on his soldiers’ shields before a battle in 312. That was the day the cross became a war flag.</li>
        <li>On his forearm is a sword inside a cross, and beside it the "<a class="aip-inline" href="/american-idolatry/dont-tread-on-me">Join, or Die</a>" snake from the American Revolution.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Hegseth is not Catholic. He belongs to a Protestant group of churches. That makes it more striking, not less: a Protestant has chosen to wear Rome’s war symbols. He wrote a book called <em>American Crusade</em>. At a prayer service at the Pentagon on March 25, 2026, during the war with Iran, he prayed, "Let every round find its mark against the enemies of righteousness and our great nation."</p>
      <p>Yahushua said the opposite. When Peter pulled a sword, He told him to put it away (Matthew 26:52). He told Pilate that His servants would not fight, because His kingdom is "not of this world" (John 18:36).</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>The cross was where Messiah died. Rome turned it into a battle flag, and now an American war leader wears it on his skin.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">The Plan</h2>
      <p>Kevin Roberts has led the Heritage Foundation, a powerful Washington think tank, since 2021. He is openly and strongly Catholic. He started a Catholic school in Louisiana and was president of Wyoming Catholic College. He is known as the father of Project 2025, a 900-page plan for how to run the government. He wrote its opening pages, and the Trump administration has put much of the plan into action.</p>
      <p>Many people helped write Project 2025, and not all of them are Catholic. But the big idea behind it is an old Catholic one: that the government should guide the nation’s family life, morals, and religion. For hundreds of years that was how Rome ruled Europe, with the church guiding the king.</p>
      <p class="aip-verdict"><strong>Most people see a policy book. Rome would see its old way of ruling, back again.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Putting the Three Together</h2>
      <p>Set the three side by side. The golden age is Rome’s promise of a kingdom on earth that lasts forever. The tattoos are Rome’s holy war, wearing the name of Messiah. The plan is Rome’s church guiding the government. Forever, holy war, and holy government all belong to Yahuah alone. Here they are being claimed by a nation.</p>
      <p>That is the idolatry this series has been tracing. Nobody has to bow to a statue. People only have to hear "golden age" and feel hope, see the Crusader cross and feel proud, and read the plan and feel safe. Without noticing, the nation takes the place of Yahuah.</p>
      <p>This is not about one party or one President. Every empire that thought it was chosen has reached for the same three things. Daniel saw a stone, cut out without hands, smash the golden statue to dust (Daniel 2:34-35). No golden age outlasts that stone.</p>
      <p>John saw the same old Rome at the end, dressed in gold and holding a golden cup, bragging, "I sit a queen" (Revelation 18:7). The answer to her has not changed.</p>
      <p class="aip-closing"><em>"Come out of her, my people" (Revelation 18:4).</em></p>
      <h2 class="aip-h2">Record Consulted</h2>
